import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="A memory that remembers — Fathom Research">
  <article class="article-wrap">
    <nav class="breadcrumb">
      <a href="/blog" data-i18n="postMemory.blog">Blog</a>
      <span class="sep">›</span>
      <span data-i18n="postMemory.crumb1">Memory</span>
      <span class="sep">›</span>
      <span data-i18n="postMemory.crumb2">Append-only knowledge</span>
    </nav>

    <div class="article-date" data-i18n="postMemory.date">August 9, 2026 · 7 min read</div>
    <span class="article-tag" data-i18n="postMemory.tag">Memory</span>
    <h1 class="article-title" data-i18n="postMemory.title">A memory that remembers: append-only knowledge for research agents</h1>

    <p class="article-intro" data-i18n="postMemory.intro">Fathom Research gives every agent an unlimited semantic knowledge base in SQLite, built on the mem0 / Memora model: append-only versioning, a six-outcome absorb pipeline, hybrid search with freshness decay, a frozen prompt snapshot that protects the prefix cache — and an entity graph of the real world right next to the facts.</p>

    <div class="highlights">
      <h4 data-i18n="postMemory.highlightsTitle">Highlights</h4>
      <ul>
        <li data-i18n-html="postMemory.hl1">Facts are never overwritten — a new version is a new record plus a <code>supersedes</code> edge</li>
        <li data-i18n="postMemory.hl2">Absorb pipeline with 6 outcomes: duplicate · supersede · contradict · coexist · related · new</li>
        <li data-i18n="postMemory.hl3">Hybrid score = 0.7·cosine + 0.3·BM25, then freshness decay of 1% per day</li>
        <li data-i18n="postMemory.hl4">A frozen memory snapshot in the system prompt keeps the LLM prefix cache intact</li>
        <li data-i18n="postMemory.hl5">person↔company entity graph with multi-hop BFS up to 4 hops</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="article-body">
      <h2 data-i18n="postMemory.h2_1">A small profile, an unlimited archive</h2>
      <p data-i18n-html="postMemory.p1">There are two memories, deliberately. The file memory — <code>MEMORY.md</code> and <code>USER.md</code> — is a stable ~2 KB "profile" that sits in the prompt. The semantic memory is the archive: every record is a self-contained fact (50–500 characters recommended) with a scope — <code>agent</code> for shared knowledge, <code>user</code> for facts about the client, <code>run</code> for session episodes. Confidence and importance scores, tags, typed edges and a full change journal sit on top, all in SQLite with FTS5 and embeddings.</p>

      <h2 data-i18n="postMemory.h2_2">Append-only, with receipts</h2>
      <p data-i18n-html="postMemory.p2">Facts are never overwritten. When something changes, the new version is a new record linked to the old one with a <code>supersedes</code> edge; contradictions stay visible from both sides through a <code>contradicts</code> edge. Reading by id has three modes: <code>active</code>, <code>latest</code> (follow the chain to the newest version) and <code>full_history</code> — the whole evolution of a fact. An audit trail is not a feature here; it is the storage model.</p>

      <h2 data-i18n="postMemory.h2_3">Absorb, not create</h2>
      <p data-i18n-html="postMemory.p3">Writing goes through a pipeline, not an insert. Validation first, then secret detection — API keys, tokens and PEM blocks are rejected outright. Nearby facts inside a batch are consolidated (N→1 at similarity 0.85), exact duplicates die by hash, and cosine similarity pulls up candidates. A classifier — the LLM when available, a 0.97-threshold heuristic otherwise — assigns each incoming fact exactly one of six outcomes: <code>duplicate</code> (skip), <code>supersede</code>, <code>contradict</code>, <code>coexist</code>, <code>related</code> or <code>new</code>. A <code>dry_run</code> mode prints the plan without writing anything.</p>

      <h2 data-i18n="postMemory.h2_4">Hybrid search with decay</h2>
      <p data-i18n-html="postMemory.p4">Retrieval mixes two signals: <code>score = 0.7·cosine + 0.3·BM25</code>. Then a linear freshness decay — <code>score × max(0, 1 − 0.01·age_in_days)</code>, one percent per day — keeps stale facts from outranking current ones. An optional second pass (<code>[memory] rerank = true</code>) lets the LLM re-order the expanded result set (top_k×3). In benchmarks the hybrid search has a median latency around 5.1 ms.</p>

      <h2 data-i18n="postMemory.h2_5">A frozen snapshot keeps the cache warm</h2>
      <p data-i18n-html="postMemory.p5">Before the session starts, the top agent receives a deterministic digest in its system prompt: relevant memories plus open TODOs plus recent records — with real ids, so the agent can verify anything via <code>memory_search</code>. Crucially, the snapshot is <em>frozen</em> for the whole session: writes are persisted immediately, but they never mutate the prompt. That keeps the LLM's prefix cache intact for the entire run; the snapshot refreshes at the next session. Memory that is cheap to read is memory that actually gets used.</p>

      <h2 data-i18n="postMemory.h2_6">A graph of the real world</h2>
      <p data-i18n-html="postMemory.p6">Alongside the facts, the store maintains an entity graph: nodes — person, company, project, technology, role, location, event, product — deduplicated by (name + type), so "Ivan Petrov" and "ivan petrov" are one node. Edges are typed — <code>works_at</code>, <code>leads</code>, <code>founded</code>, <code>located_in</code> — and multi-hop BFS up to 4 hops answers questions like "who leads X" or "who works at companies in Kazan". When an absorbed fact carries entity metadata, its nodes and edges are created with it, automatically.</p>

      <blockquote data-i18n-html="postMemory.quote">
        Nothing is ever deleted. Distillation compresses, GC archives, and the full history stays reachable by id — a memory you can audit is a memory you can trust.
        <cite>— Fathom Research, memory design notes</cite>
      </blockquote>

      <h2 data-i18n="postMemory.h2_7">Housekeeping without deletion</h2>
      <p data-i18n-html="postMemory.p7">Nightly-style consolidation is <code>distill</code>: run-facts from sessions are re-absorbed into durable agent knowledge, duplicates filtered out, originals archived. <code>gc</code> is a conservative offline sweep in three stages — expire rows past their TTL, archive stale never-accessed run-facts, and compact overgrown scope groups into consolidated summaries. Both are available from the CLI and over HTTP, both support <code>--dry-run</code>, and neither ever destroys a record. Absorb takes 49–521 µs per fact and re-absorbing 100 already-known facts is a 5.1 ms dedup fast path — the store is cheap enough to be always on.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="related-posts">
      <h3 data-i18n="postMemory.related">Related Posts</h3>
      <div class="related-grid">
        <a class="related-card" href="/blog/benchmarks-repo-map" data-i18n="postMemory.related1">The benchmark that caught a 31× slowdown</a>
        <a class="related-card" href="/blog/anatomy-of-fan-out" data-i18n="postMemory.related2">Anatomy of a fan-out: one question, a fleet of agents</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .article-wrap { max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 80px 24px; }

  .breadcrumb { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 13px; color: var(--grey-600); margin-bottom: 32px; flex-wrap: wrap; }
  .breadcrumb a { color: var(--grey-600); transition: color .15s; }
  .breadcrumb a:hover { color: var(--grey-900); }
  .breadcrumb .sep { color: var(--grey-400); }

  .article-date { font-size: 15px; color: var(--grey-600); margin-bottom: 14px; }
  .article-tag { display: inline-block; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 11px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .6px; color: var(--grey-600); background: var(--grey-100); border: 1px solid var(--grey-200); padding: 4px 12px; border-radius: 100px; margin-bottom: 20px; }
  .article-title { font-size: clamp(28px, 4vw, 44px); line-height: 1.1; letter-spacing: -.025em; font-weight: 500; }
  .article-intro { margin-top: 28px; font-size: 18px; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--grey-700); }

  .highlights { margin: 36px 0; background: var(--grey-50); border: 1px solid var(--grey-200); border-radius: 10px; padding: 24px 28px; }
  .highlights h4 { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 12px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .6px; color: var(--grey-600); margin-bottom: 14px; }
  .highlights ul { list-style: none; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 10px; }
  .highlights li { position: relative; padding-left: 18px; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.55; color: var(--grey-800); }
  .highlights li::before { content: ""; position: absolute; left: 0; top: 8px; width: 6px; height: 6px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--grey-900); }
  .highlights code { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: .85em; background: var(--bg-card); border: 1px solid var(--grey-200); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 5px; }

  .article-body h2 { font-size: clamp(20px, 2.8vw, 28px); margin: 48px 0 16px; letter-spacing: -.02em; line-height: 1.15; }
  .article-body p { font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--grey-800); margin-bottom: 18px; }
  .article-body code { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: .85em; background: var(--grey-100); border: 1px solid var(--grey-200); padding: 1px 6px; border-radius: 5px; }

  .article-body blockquote { margin: 40px 0; padding: 28px 32px; border-left: 3px solid #181818; background: var(--bg-card); border-radius: 0 10px 10px 0; font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.6; color: var(--grey-800); font-style: italic; }
  .article-body blockquote cite { display: block; margin-top: 18px; font-style: normal; font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 13px; color: var(--grey-600); }

  .related-posts { margin-top: 56px; padding-top: 32px; border-top: 1px solid var(--grey-200); }
  .related-posts h3 { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 12px; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .6px; color: var(--grey-600); margin-bottom: 16px; }
  .related-grid { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 12px; }
  .related-card { display: block; border: 1px solid var(--grey-200); border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px 20px; background: var(--bg-card); font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--grey-800); transition: border-color .15s, color .15s; }
  .related-card:hover { border-color: var(--grey-500); color: var(--grey-900); }
</style>
